---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How ControlCurrent maps, normalises, and evaluates browser compatibility evidence."
>
  <header class="shell page-header">
    <span class="eyebrow">Methodology</span>
    <h1>A qualified answer is more useful than a confident guess</h1>
    <p>
      ControlCurrent selects a bounded security-specific subset of MDN BCD, validates every
      configured path, and preserves the conditions attached to support statements.
    </p>
  </header>
  <article class="shell prose">
    <h2>Evidence layers</h2>
    <p>The application keeps six statements separate:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Source data:</strong> BCD paths, browser statements, versions, and notes.</li>
      <li><strong>Catalogue mapping:</strong> which source paths represent a security control.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculation:</strong> whether a selected baseline meets every required path.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Guidance:</strong> project-authored prerequisites, fallbacks, and limitations.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Observed configuration:</strong> optional local evidence from supplied response, HTML
        resource, request-context, and reduced WebAuthn observations.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Conformance context:</strong> exact reviewed WPT suite paths and explicit limits.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Mapping rule</h2>
    <p>
      A control may require one path or several. An <code>all</code> mapping is available only when every
      path is available. A control remains <code>without a standalone BCD mapping</code> when BCD cannot
      support the intended claim. CSP nonces and CSP hashes are intentionally in that state in the current
      catalogue, while the local evidence inspector can still identify their deployment syntax in an applicable
      enforced CSP source list.
    </p>

    <h2>Support normalisation</h2>
    <p>
      Support versions are compared as browser release version components, not floating-point
      numbers. Exact support, removal, last-supported versions, imprecise upper-bound records, and
      multiple statements are handled separately. Flags, partial implementations, prefixes,
      alternative names, and notes produce a qualified result rather than disappearing into a
      boolean.
    </p>

    <h2>Outcome meanings</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Available:</strong> every required path is available without a recorded qualification.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Qualified:</strong> available, with at least one source qualification.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unavailable:</strong> a required path is not supported at the selected minimum.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Removed:</strong> a required path was removed by the selected minimum.</li>
      <li><strong>Unknown:</strong> the source cannot support a comparable conclusion.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>No standalone BCD mapping:</strong> the catalogue deliberately refuses a compatibility
        claim that BCD cannot express at the required granularity.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Source issue:</strong> the selected source subset and catalogue are inconsistent.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Time semantics</h2>
    <p>
      The change feed records the BCD package timestamp and the version in which ControlCurrent
      first observed a selected-subset change. It does not claim that a browser implementation
      changed at that exact time.
    </p>

    <h2>Offline deployment assessment</h2>
    <p>
      The inspector parses one bounded, user-supplied final response. It makes no URL request and
      reports only redacted evidence. “Observed” means recognizable syntax was present in an
      applicable enforced policy or header in that response; it does not prove browser enforcement,
      coverage across routes, rollout safety, or runtime behaviour. Report-only policy, incompatible
      multiple-policy evidence, and controls requiring another evidence surface remain explicitly
      separate. A bounded bundle can add CSP-to-markup correlation, local SRI byte verification,
      Fetch Metadata, reduced WebAuthn evidence, declared surface coverage, and deterministic
      reduced-report comparison while preserving those limits.
    </p>

    <h2>Conformance evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Each control has one mapped or explicitly unmapped WPT evidence record. Repository links are
      pinned to the reviewed source revision. Current wpt.fyi links are investigation aids;
      ControlCurrent does not ingest pass rates or use them to change compatibility outcomes.
      Conformance coverage does not prove correct application configuration.
    </p>

    <h2>Determinism and bounds</h2>
    <p>
      The locked package produces a canonical selected snapshot, schema fingerprint, sorted release
      list, versioned catalogue mapping, and deterministic event identifiers. The importer caps
      paths, statements, releases, strings, events, stored profiles, and exports. A missing
      configured path fails generation instead of silently becoming available.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      Browser compatibility cannot establish correct application configuration, complete
      specification conformance, freedom from browser defects, or the adequacy of an organisation’s
      security policy.
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
